# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.
#
# Patterns here are deliberately unanchored. This was a single-app repository
# and every rule below started with a leading slash, which matches the repo root
# and nothing else — the moment the app moved to apps/ttgamba/ they all silently
# stopped matching and node_modules, build output and the generated Prisma
# client became committable. Keep new rules unanchored unless a rule genuinely
# only ever applies at the root.

# dependencies
node_modules
.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
coverage

# next.js
.next/
out/

# production
build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# The template carries no secrets and is what a new checkout copies from.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# turbo
.turbo

# prisma
# The local dev database. Migrations are the source of truth for its shape, so
# a checked-in binary would only ever be one contributor's copy of the data.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
# Generated from schema.prisma by `prisma generate`, which `postinstall` runs.
# A pattern with a slash in it is anchored no matter where the slash falls, so
# `lib/generated` would only ever have matched the old root-level app.
**/lib/generated
